.TH ORCHARD 1
.SH NAME
orchard \- a maze chase
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B games/orchard
[
.B -t
|
.B -s
] [
.B -q
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Orchard
is a chase in the shape that genre took:
a maze of berries to clear,
four chasers that each want somewhere different,
four bigger berries that turn them blue and edible for a while,
and a tunnel across the middle row that both sides can use.
Eating a frightened chaser is worth 200, then 400, then 800, then 1600
until the fright wears off; it goes back to its pen as a pair of eyes
and comes out again.
Clearing the maze advances the level and shortens the fright.
.PP
What this exercises in
.IR nes (2)
that the other two do not is per-tile reasoning.
Everything moves on the grid and may only change direction at a tile's
centre, and a chaser picks its direction each time it reaches one by
scoring the exits \- all but the one it came from \- against a target
square, ties going to a fixed order of up, left, down, right so a chase
is repeatable rather than a coin toss.
The target is the only thing that differs between the four:
one comes straight at you,
one aims four squares in front of you,
one mirrors the first about a point ahead of you so it closes from the
far side,
and one gives up and goes home when it gets close.
They alternate between chasing and scattering to their own corners, and
turn round when that changes, which is what stops one tailing you down a
corridor for ever.
.PP
Arrow keys or
.BR wasd .
A direction is remembered until it can be taken, so a turn may be asked
for before the corner.
.B q
or Escape quits.
.PP
.B -q
starts silent.
.B -t
runs the self test.
.B -s
plays a scripted opening headless and writes the frame to
.BR orchard.raw .
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
games/orchard
emu\-cocoa \-r. /dis/games/orchard.dis \-t
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/games/orchard.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR nes (2),
.IR rivet (1),
.IR sprocket (1),
.IR starling (1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B -t
drives the same
.I step
the game loop drives and asserts on game state.
It checks the maze's shape and that it fits the screen;
that the player starts on a tile centre and returns to one;
that a wall stops it rather than letting it through;
that berries are eaten, the count agrees with the maze afterwards, and
eating scores;
that a big berry frightens all four chasers at once;
that eating a frightened one is worth 200 then 400 and turns it to eyes,
and that an unfrightened one costs a life;
that the four want four different squares and the first wants the
player's own;
that a chaser released from the pen gets out of it;
that the tunnel wraps both ways;
and that an empty maze advances the level and refills.
.SH BUGS
There is one maze.
Levels differ only in how long a big berry lasts.
.PP
A frightened chaser aims at the opposite corner rather than fleeing the
player directly, so it can run towards you across a room on its way
there.
.PP
.I Newround
deliberately does not rebuild the maze \- that is what keeps a lost life
from refilling it \- which means a test that wants berries where the
player starts has to ask for both.
Two checks failed for that reason before it was noticed, and read
exactly like a broken pickup.
